let's talk about ukraine and the threat  it's facing from russia  president vladimir putin has been  building up his forces along the border  there are now tens of thousands of  soldiers there nato was alarmed about  russia's military buildup on ukraine's  borders new intelligence suggests the  kremlin is in advance stages of planning  an invasion of ukraine russia the u.s  and other big players have been talking  the people are still nervous any  provocation from any side can be a  trigger for more serious conflict so  what's behind all this to understand the  story here are five things you need to  know  okay so let's start with the basics  here's ukraine it's sandwiched between  russia on one side belarus to the north  and the rest of europe on the other side  and that geography puts ukraine on the  front line in a big power play between  russia and the west  and here's the first thing to know about  what's going on right now it looks like  russia is ready for a fight  all along the border with ukraine there  are around 100 000 russian soldiers  hundreds of tanks and lots of heavy  artillery when i talk to russian  military experts they say that there's  missiles in places where they shouldn't  be and that tanks are fully dressed and  this idiom doesn't mean that the tanks  have lipstick on or their earrings on it  means that they have metal cages on and  they're ready for war we are very very  concerned  but we are not panicking we live with  russian threat for almost eight years  so army is preparing  but definitely you know it's not very  comfortable thing is the relationship  between russia and ukraine has not been  very comfortable for a while now and  that's putting it mildly so here's the  second thing you need to know there's  history between these two this is the  quick version  ukraine and russia were both part of the  soviet union  when the ussr broke up in 1991 they  became separate independent countries  russia was the most powerful country in  the region and still is russia has a  very parochial view of ukraine russia  sees ukraine as its little brother  ukraine was part of the soviet union of  course uh but ukraine has its own  independent identity and part of what  we're seeing here and part of what we've  been seeing since  2013 it is  a so moscow does not want to let ukraine  go so what happened in 2013  well that year kicked off a really  tumultuous time for ukraine it was  tested would it lean more towards its  european neighbors in the west or stay  within russia's orbit  the president back then viktor  yanukovych was about to sign a political  and economic deal with eu but instead he  signed a deal with moscow which a lot of  ukrainians saw as a sign of pressure  from russia  there were huge protests that went on  for months yanukovych was kicked out and  the eu deal was eventually signed by a  new president it was a protest against  attempts to  impose authoritarian model on ukraine  now while all this was happening in  early 2014 president putin made a big  move he sent forces into crimea it's a  peninsula that had been part of ukraine  since the 1950s but russian speakers are  a majority of the population and crimea  always had close ties with russia there  was a referendum in crimea which ukraine  in the west said was illegal but the  people there voted to join russia and  the next thing we knew russia had  annexed crimea the referendum in crimea  was a clear violation of ukrainian  and  international law nobody predicted in  2014 that russia would attack ukraine it  happened but it didn't end with crimea  because things also kicked off in  eastern ukraine we're talking about the  donbass region it's made up of luhansk  and donetsk and is a part of ukraine  that has a big population of ethnic  russians in april 2014 pro-russian  separatists started taking territory and  the fighting became a full-scale war  between them and the ukrainian military  the separatists took control of two  areas they named them the people's  republics of luhansk and donetsk and  said they were independent from ukraine  russia was accused of backing the  separatists with troops and weapons  although moscow has always denied it  eight years later diplomatic initiatives  to resolve the crisis have basically  failed more than 14 000 people have been  killed with 1.5 million forced to leave  their homes the status of eastern  ukraine still hangs on the balance and  the fighting hasn't stopped so that's  the history lesson which helps to  explain where we are now  but then there's nato and that's the  next thing you need to know about with  everything that's happened in the last  eight years ukraine's been getting a lot  of military support from the west it's  now closer than ever to nato and russia  doesn't like it  in washington the signing ceremony of  the north atlantic treaty now remember  nato is the military alliance founded by  western countries after world war ii to  band together against the soviet union  since the collapse of the soviet union  nato's expanded eastward nato now  includes countries that russia sees as  being in its backyard like poland  bulgaria and the baltic states  and russia believes that's a threat to  its own security  it doesn't want ukraine to be next on  the list even though most experts think  that's pretty unlikely anyway  now putin recently made some pretty big  demands which he wants as legal  guarantees among other things he wants  nato to stop any kind of military  cooperation with ukraine and to rule out  nato membership for ukraine or anyone  else and that's a red line we know that  the west will not agree to that  washington can't agree to that russia is  demanding  very very  complicated thing putin is trying not to  uh speak about  russia's proposal as an ultimate  but in fact it's an ultimate so putin's  made those big demands and he has all  those troops on the border but what's  his actual strategy well the next thing  you need to know is that some people  think there's a lot more going on here  people uh want to understand the logic  of putin and it's still not clear one  theory is that this is an elaborate  bluff putin is trying to scare the heck  out of everyone he's trying to scare  biden he's trying to scare the  ukrainians and he's trying to scare  western europe into a deal  another theory is that this isn't just  about nato and military security it's  also about putin's own political  security if ukraine looks like a  successful alternative to his way of  running a country well maybe that makes  putin more vulnerable putin is afraid of  democracy and freedoms and you have  ukraine  on his borders where many russians  speaking ukrainians many russians lives  and it it's it's a free country  so this was interpreted by putin as an  existential threat  to his authoritarian regime if ukraine  succeeds then all of his excuses are  invalid russia and ukraine are are  similar enough that he won't be able to  say uh that democracy is impossible in  russia right okay so here's the last  thing you need to know the current state  of play  the u.s and its allies have been  threatening tough sanctions against  putin if he does go ahead and invade  ukraine they'd be designed to crush  russia's economy and hit its tech and  defense sectors aggression against  ukraine will be met with massive  consequences but some experts question  whether sanctions are much of a  deterrent and think russia is already  making a plan for how it would deal with  them  now the u.s and russia and others have  been holding talks the idea is to calm  things down and avoid a war but no one  seems to be budging we underscore that  for us it's absolutely mandatory  to make sure that  ukraine never  never ever  becomes member of nato russia has to  make a stark choice de-escalation and  diplomacy or confrontation and  consequences one thing's for sure this  is a high-stakes situation for russia  for the u.s and nato and especially for  the people of ukraine the big question  is what's putin's next move  i know him for many many years he will  be tough in the end  he will be tough till the  real red line  and this red line is ukraine  [Music]
 

